Implementing multiple-choice dialogue in a text-based Java game is a common task. The best approach depends on the complexity of your game.

Here are the three most common methods, ranging from simple to advanced.

### Method 1: The "Switch-Case" Approach (Simplest)
This is best for games with a linear story or a small number of dialogue options. You store the text in a list and use a `switch` statement to handle the user's input.

**Pros:** Very easy to read and write.
**Cons:** Hard to manage if you have hundreds of dialogue lines or need to save/load progress.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class DialogueSystem {
    private Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

    public void showDialogue(String characterName, String[] options) {
        System.out.println("\n--- " + characterName + " says:");
        System.out.println("Hello! How can I help you?");

        System.out.println("\nChoose an option:");
        for (int i = 0; i < options.length; i++) {
            System.out.println((i + 1) + ". " + options[i]);
        }

        System.out.print("\nEnter choice: ");
        int choice = scanner.nextInt();

        // Handle the choice
        switch (choice) {
            case 1:
                System.out.println("You chose: " + options[0]);
                // Add logic here (e.g., update inventory, change story path)
                break;
            case 2:
                System.out.println("You chose: " + options[1]);
                break;
            case 3:
                System.out.println("You chose: " + options[2]);
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println("Invalid choice. Please try again.");
                // You might want to call showDialogue again here
                break;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DialogueSystem game = new DialogueSystem();
        String[] options = {"Attack", "Defend", "Run Away"};
        game.showDialogue("Goblin", options);
    }
}
```

